                    "messages": [
                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-0",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round 2: replaced the 4-kernel alpha-add + in-place chain of candidate-01 with a 3-kernel algebraically equivalent form. Since 1.25>0, relu(1.25*(z-0.1)) == 1.25*relu(z-0.1), and relu(z-0.1) == clamp_min(z,0.1)-0.1, so the operator reduces to clamp_min(x+0.6y, 0.1)*1.25 - 0.125. The final scale+shift is done with a single torch.add that puts a cached 0-dim constant in the broadcast `input` slot and the running tensor in the `other` slot, so alpha=1.25 scales the tensor — one kernel instead of mul_+sub_. Net: 3 device kernels (was 4 in candidate-01, 6 in baseline), one allocation, no clone, inputs untouched. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline; only run(inputs) changed.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Latency is kernel-launch bound, not bandwidth bound: 32x256 FP32 is 32KB, so each elementwise kernel costs ~2.5us of launch/dispatch on the shared GPU. Measured evidence: baseline 15.36us with 6 kernels -> candidate-01 10.24us with 4 kernels, i.e. ~2.56us per eliminated kernel. The only lever that moves p50 materially is reducing kernel count further, so this round removes one more kernel and keeps everything else (tolerances, inputs, oracle) fixed.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"per-kernel launch/dispatch overhead (~2.5us/kernel at this shape) dominates over memory traffic\",\"redundant clone of the relu result (removed in round 1)\",\"separate mul_ and sub_ kernels for the affine tail (removed this round)\",\"per-call construction of a scalar shift tensor would add a host-to-device copy, so it is cached by (device, dtype)\"]},\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"Collapse the affine tail to a single broadcast add-with-alpha and relu to clamp_min (4 kernels -> 3)\",\"hypothesis\":\"p50 scales with kernel count at this size. Folding scale+shift into one torch.add(shift_const, z, alpha=1.25) kernel and expressing relu(z-0.1) as clamp_min(z,0.1) removes one of candidate-01's four kernels while staying mathematically identical, so p50 should drop by roughly one kernel's worth (~2.5us) versus candidate-01 and by ~7.7us versus the 15.36us baseline.\",\"change\":\"In run.py only: `value = torch.add(inputs['x'], inputs['y'], alpha=0.6)`; `torch.clamp_min_(value, 0.1)`; `return torch.add(_shift(value.device, value.dtype), value, alpha=1.25)`. `_shift` lazily caches a 0-dim tensor(-0.125) keyed by (device, dtype) so no per-call H2D copy occurs. Derivation: 1.25x+0.75y-0.125 == 1.25*(x+0.6y-0.1); relu(1.25*t)==1.25*relu(t) for 1.25>0; relu(z-0.1)==clamp_min(z,0.1)-0.1. For z>=0.1 the result is exactly 1.25z-0.125 (the pre-relu value is non-negative there, so relu is the identity); for z<0.1 it is 1.25*0.1-0.125, which is exactly 0 in float32 (1.25f*0.1f rounds to 0.125f). clamp_min_ operates on the freshly allocated add() result, so inputs['x']/inputs['y'] are never mutated and no defensive clone is needed.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"Algebraic refactor changes rounding by ~1 ULP-level amount; expected maxDiff stays near the 9.5e-7 seen in round 1, well inside atol=rtol=1e-5\",\"The broadcast-input form torch.add(0-dim const, tensor, alpha=1.25) relies on alpha scaling `other`; if a dispatch path ever scaled `input` instead the result would be wrong, so this is the main thing the correctness run must confirm\",\"Cached 0-dim constant is created on first call (allocated device/dtype of the running tensor), so no device or dtype assumption is baked in\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02 on the shared GPU for correctness + full benchmark\",\"reason\":\"Round 1 proved the refactor direction is correct (4/4 on both profiles, maxDiff 9.5e-7) but landed at 10.24us against a 15.36us baseline. This candidate is a genuinely different, smaller Diff that removes one more kernel launch.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness on primary and small profiles, plus p50_us/p95_us/min_us/max_us for both profiles, so the kernel-count hypothesis can be checked against real numbers rather than assumed.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Absolute target of 99.9999% relative improvement (sub-nanosecond latency) is unreachable for any real GPU kernel chain; the achievable result is a bounded constant-factor reduction in p50\",\"Shared-GPU noise between queue runs may be comparable to the expected ~2.5us gain, so the improvement may not be cleanly separable in a single measurement\"]}",
                        "time": "刚刚"
                      }
                    ],
