                      "format": "structured-json",
                      "summary": "Round 2: replaced the 4-kernel alpha-add + in-place chain of candidate-01 with a 3-kernel algebraically equivalent form. Since 1.25>0, relu(1.25*(z-0.1)) == 1.25*relu(z-0.1), and relu(z-0.1) == clamp_min(z,0.1)-0.1, so the operator reduces to clamp_min(x+0.6y, 0.1)*1.25 - 0.125. The final scale+shift is done with a single torch.add that puts a cached 0-dim constant in the broadcast `input` slot and the running tensor in the `other` slot, so alpha=1.25 scales the tensor — one kernel instead of mul_+sub_. Net: 3 device kernels (was 4 in candidate-01, 6 in baseline), one allocation, no clone, inputs untouched. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline; only run(inputs) changed.",
                      "diagnosis": {
                        "summary": "Latency is kernel-launch bound, not bandwidth bound: 32x256 FP32 is 32KB, so each elementwise kernel costs ~2.5us of launch/dispatch on the shared GPU. Measured evidence: baseline 15.36us with 6 kernels -> candidate-01 10.24us with 4 kernels, i.e. ~2.56us per eliminated kernel. The only lever that moves p50 materially is reducing kernel count further, so this round removes one more kernel and keeps everything else (tolerances, inputs, oracle) fixed.",
                        "bottlenecks": [
                          "per-kernel launch/dispatch overhead (~2.5us/kernel at this shape) dominates over memory traffic",
                          "redundant clone of the relu result (removed in round 1)",
                          "separate mul_ and sub_ kernels for the affine tail (removed this round)",
                          "per-call construction of a scalar shift tensor would add a host-to-device copy, so it is cached by (device, dtype)"
                        ]
                      },
