              "candidateEvaluations": [
                {
                  "id": "candidate-02",
                  "version": "agent.2",
                  "label": "Collapse the affine tail to a single broadcast add-with-alpha and relu to clamp_min (4 kernels -> 3)",
                  "title": "Collapse the affine tail to a single broadcast add-with-alpha and relu to clamp_min (4 kernels -> 3)",
                  "hypothesis": "p50 scales with kernel count at this size. Folding scale+shift into one torch.add(shift_const, z, alpha=1.25) kernel and expressing relu(z-0.1) as clamp_min(z,0.1) removes one of candidate-01's four kernels while staying mathematically identical, so p50 should drop by roughly one kernel's worth (~2.5us) versus candidate-01 and by ~7.7us versus the 15.36us baseline.",
                  "change": "In run.py only: `value = torch.add(inputs['x'], inputs['y'], alpha=0.6)`; `torch.clamp_min_(value, 0.1)`; `return torch.add(_shift(value.device, value.dtype), value, alpha=1.25)`. `_shift` lazily caches a 0-dim tensor(-0.125) keyed by (device, dtype) so no per-call H2D copy occurs. Derivation: 1.25x+0.75y-0.125 == 1.25*(x+0.6y-0.1); relu(1.25*t)==1.25*relu(t) for 1.25>0; relu(z-0.1)==clamp_min(z,0.1)-0.1. For z>=0.1 the result is exactly 1.25z-0.125 (the pre-relu value is non-negative there, so relu is the identity); for z<0.1 it is 1.25*0.1-0.125, which is exactly 0 in float32 (1.25f*0.1f rounds to 0.125f). clamp_min_ operates on the freshly allocated add() result, so inputs['x']/inputs['y'] are never mutated and no defensive clone is needed.",
                  "files": "run.py",
                  "status": "弱候选参考",
                  "classification": "reference",
