                    "title": "Drop redundant clone and fuse scalar ops into one alpha-add + in-place chain",
                    "direction": "In run.py, replace `value = torch.relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125); return value.clone()` with: value = torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6) (single fused kernel for x + 0.6*y, exactly 1.25x + 0.75y after the following scale), then value.mul_(1.25), value.sub_(0.125), return torch.relu_(value). All in-place ops operate on the freshly allocated add() result, so the caller's inputs['x']/inputs['y'] are never mutated and no clone is needed. The algebraic refactor 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 == 1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125 is exact in real arithmetic; the only difference is float rounding well inside the 1e-5 tolerance. get_inputs, get_test_cases, get_benchmark_inputs and reference are untouched.",
